title: Atomic Environments
keyword: ENVIRON
manual: structure/#c2-environ
cite: econ_1979 econ_2016

Lists the neighbors of one atom of the system in the "System" line: their distances, whether
they are coordinated to it, and their weights in its effective coordination number (ECoN). It
brings together, for one atom at a time, what the ENVIRON, ECON and COORD keywords calculate
for all of them. The atom and its coordinated neighbors are marked in the view while the window
is open, and the list is recalculated whenever the atom, the options or the geometry change.

# Options

- "Atom": the atom whose environment is shown, chosen from the list of non-equivalent atoms.
  "Pick" lets you click it in the view instead; in a crystal, picking also chooses which image
  of the atom is marked. "Cell atom" and "Lattice vector" give the atom of the unit cell and the
  image.
- "Up to": list the neighbors up to a distance from the atom (in Å), or up to a number of
  shells. A shell is the group of neighbors at the same distance, which may be of different
  kinds.
- "Species": list only the neighbors of one species.
- "Group by shells": one row per shell and atom (the atoms of a shell that are images of the
  same non-equivalent atom), with their number ("Count") and how many of them are
  coordinated.
- "Show in view": mark the atom (orange halo) and its coordinated neighbors (blue halos) in the
  view. The neighbors of the row under the mouse are marked in yellow, with a line from the
  atom.

# Results

The "Neighbors" table has one row per neighbor, sorted by distance: its shell, name, index in
the list of non-equivalent atoms ("Id") and in the unit cell ("Cell id"), the lattice vector of
its image next to the marked atom (crystals), the distance, whether it is coordinated (bonded
to the atom in the bond list of the system, which the bonds objects draw unless they use their
own criteria), and its ECoN weight, exp(1 - (d/d_av)^6), where d_av is the mean weighted
distance of the environment of the atom (Hoppe's formula, iterated as in ECON). Neighbors with
a weight of at least 0.5 count as coordinated in the integer ECoN. Right-click the table header
to copy the table or write it to a file.
